    "summary": "Acoustic gems and a fiery Other One‑Lovelight stretch",
    "blurb": "The set kicks off with an intimate acoustic segment that includes Friend Of The Devil, Deep Elem Blues and the debut of Candyman. A high‑energy surge from The Other One through Turn On Your Lovelight keeps the momentum rolling, while Good Lovin' highlights tight drumming and Pigpen’s soulful contributions.",
    "review": "April 3, 1970 at the Field House, University of Cincinnati captures a Grateful Dead in transition – acoustic intimacy meets electric fire. Jerry Garcia shines in the early acoustic numbers, delivering an especially moving Friend Of The Devil and sweet‑toned vocals on Deep Elem Blues and Candyman, the latter making its first ever appearance. The band then builds into a seamless high‑energy stretch: The Other One morphs into Turn On Your Lovelight with that classic mid‑show surge that Deadheads love. Good Lovin' is a standout groove showcase – solid, uninterrupted drumming from Mickey and Bill underpins Pigpen’s gritty keyboard/harmonica flavor, while the dual Good Lovin' reprise adds extra punch. Throughout, Phil Lesh locks in with Jerry, laying down a sturdy bass foundation, and Bob Weir contributes tasteful rhythm work, especially on the acoustic Wake Up Little Susie. The performance is consistently strong, making this show a must‑listen for anyone chasing early‑’70 Dead magic.",
